{"answer":"There are 7 subsets:\n{1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.\n\nFor example, {1,8} has sum 9. Exhaustiveness follows by cardinality: no singleton is possible because 9 is absent; the four two-element cases and three three-element cases are exactly those listed; and any subset of four or more distinct positive elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10.","approaches":[{"description":"Enumerate qualifying subsets by increasing cardinality, always writing their elements in increasing order.","id":"cardinality-case-split","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It produces a complete finite case split and an explicit lower bound rules out every cardinality at least four."},{"description":"List arbitrary subsets first and retain those whose sums equal 9.","id":"unstructured-subset-listing","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It can find the answer but does not by itself give a concise, checkable exhaustiveness argument."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question specifies subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} with sum 9.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"problem-domain","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The objects under consideration are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the cardinality cases in steps singleton-case through large-cardinality-case.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["problem-domain"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-enumeration","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The qualifying subsets are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["singleton-case","pair-case","triple-case","large-cardinality-case"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The seven distinct subsets in complete-enumeration are the complete list.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["complete-enumeration"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["count-listed-subsets"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct addition.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["complete-enumeration"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"exhibited-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is one qualifying subset, since 1+8=9.","step_ids":["exhibit-subset"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"singleton-case","justification":"A singleton with sum 9 would have to be {9}, but 9 is not an element of the specified ground set.","statement":"There are no qualifying singleton subsets."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"pair-case","justification":"For an increasing pair {a,b}, a+b=9. Taking a=1,2,3,4 gives respectively b=8,7,6,5. If a>=5, then b>a implies a+b>=5+6>9. Thus these four are all increasing pairs.","statement":"The qualifying two-element subsets are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"triple-case","justification":"Write an increasing triple as {a,b,c}. If a=1, then b+c=8 with 1<b<c, giving {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}; b=4 would force c=4, not distinct. If a=2, then b+c=7 with 2<b<c, giving {2,3,4}. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12>9. Hence the list is complete.","statement":"The qualifying three-element subsets are exactly {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"large-cardinality-case","justification":"Any subset with at least four distinct positive elements has sum at least the sum of the four least available elements, 1+2+3+4=10, which exceeds 9.","statement":"No qualifying subset has four or more elements."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["singleton-case","pair-case","triple-case","large-cardinality-case"],"id":"count-listed-subsets","justification":"The cardinality cases are disjoint 